      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "©2017 The Author(s).",
        "AbstractText": "MicroRNAs (miRNAs) play an essential role in articular cartilage development and growth. However, the exact mechanisms involved in this process remain unknown. In this study, we investigated the biological functions of miRNA-27b (miR-27b) during hypertrophic differentiation of rat articular chondrocytes. Based on in situ hybridization and immunohistochemistry, we report that miR-27b expression is reduced in the hypertrophic zone of articular cartilage, but expression of peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor-γ (Pparγ) is increased. Dual-luciferase reporter gene assay and western blot analysis demonstrated that Pparγ2 is a target of miR-27b. Overexpression of miR-27b inhibited expression of Pparγ2, as well as type X collagen (Col10a1) and matrix metalloproteinase 13, while significantly promoting expression of SRY-box 9 and type II collagen (Col2a1) at both the mRNA and protein levels. Rosiglitazone, a Pparγ agonist, suppressed Col2a1 expression, while promoting expression of runt-related transcription factor 2 and Col10a1 in a concentration-dependent manner. Small interfering RNA (siRNA) mediated knockdown of Pparγ caused an increase of protein levels of Col2a1. This study demonstrates that miR-27b regulates chondrocyte hypertrophy in part by targeting Pparγ2, and that miR-27b may have important therapeutic implications in cartilage diseases."
      },
